window.docContents[206].resolve({"/Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template/What-the-machine-can-hold-here/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template--What-the-machine-can-hold-here":{"contents":"Of the 17 rubrics, 7 have no census and can have none — their population is what the document says. Of the other 10, 5 are walked today (2 by an audit that fails the build, the rest by a generated view or a pinned reading), and 5 have a population a sweep could enumerate and no sweep walking it: for those, the template is reporting exemplars, and is only as complete as the author's memory.\n\nThe framing is the model template's — a census needs a population that is a type, a\ndecidable predicate on its members, and a way to declare the honest exceptions — but this\ntemplate's profile differs in kind, for the reason the chapter opened with. Almost\neverything a deployment adds is unproved by construction: an image is built, a queue is\nchosen, an ABI floor is what it is. More of this template is therefore prose, and a\ndocument that states those facts plainly has done what can be done about them.\n\nWhat is worth seeing in the table below is that the rows reading census possible are\nnearly all one move from being held, and it is the same move each time. The sizing\nrecords, the sweep behind each operating point, the settable knobs, the paired run behind\na performance claim — every one of them is data the deployment already has and currently\nwrites into a sentence. Declared instead of narrated, each becomes a population something\ncan walk, which is the step M9 took for crossings in the model template and the reason\nthat rubric gates where its neighbours only report.\n\n* * Closure* What holds the population, or what a sweep would walk* What is deployed* * * D1* no census* —* D2* no census* —* The contracts* * * D3* census possible* declared payload faces with no validation ahead of the first decode* D4* gated* #kind_discharges_decide c d* D5* pinned* the deployment card, generated per declared unit* Sizing and knobs* * * D6* census possible* sizing constants, once the measured records behind each are declared* D7* census possible* reported operating points, once the sweep behind each is declared* D8* census possible* settable environment knobs, once declared as data rather than listed in prose* The platform* * * D9* no census* —* D10* pinned* the deployed version and registration payload, derived from the artifact* D11* no census* —* Acceptance* * * D12* gated* the acceptance run itself, executed against the model document's specification* D13* census possible* performance claims, once each names the paired run in the deployed executable behind it* D14* no census* —* Reproducibility and limits* * * D15* no census* —* D16* pinned* the manifest, generated from the artifacts that ran* D17* no census* —\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Deploying a domain model: the deployment template","header":"35.8. What the machine can hold here","id":"/Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template/What-the-machine-can-hold-here/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Deploying-a-domain-model___-the-deployment-template--What-the-machine-can-hold-here"},"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/Item-index-___-IEC-80000-13/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--Item-index-___-IEC-80000-13":{"contents":"Every catalogued item of IEC 80000-13, indexed by its printed item number, with the\nprincipal quantity symbol, the unit (the special named unit where the standard gives one),\nand the PhysLib dimension this work assigns it. Each item number links to the formalized\nresult it participates in. Symbols and unit strings are citation locators; nothing\nnormative is reproduced.\n\n* Item* Quantity* Symbol* Unit* Dimension* 13-1* traffic intensity* A* E* 1* 13-2* traffic offered intensity* A_o* E* 1* 13-3* traffic carried intensity* Y* E* 1* 13-4* mean queue length* L* 1* 1* 13-5* loss probability* B* 1* 1* 13-6* waiting probability* W* 1* 1* 13-7* call intensity* λ* s⁻¹* T⁻¹* 13-8* completed call intensity* μ* s⁻¹* T⁻¹* 13-9* storage capacity* M* bit* 1* 13-10* equivalent binary storage capacity* M_e* bit* 1* 13-11* transfer rate* r* s⁻¹* T⁻¹* 13-12* period of data elements* T* s* T* 13-13* binary digit rate* r_bit* bit/s* T⁻¹* 13-14* period of binary digits* T_bit* s* T* 13-15* equivalent binary digit rate* r_e* bit/s* T⁻¹* 13-16* modulation rate* r_m* Bd* T⁻¹* 13-17* quantizing distortion* T_Q* W* M·L²·T⁻³* 13-18* carrier power* P_c* W* M·L²·T⁻³* 13-19* signal energy per binary digit* E_bit* J* M·L²·T⁻²* 13-20* error probability* P* 1* 1* 13-21* Hamming distance* d_n* 1* 1* 13-22* clock frequency* f_cl* Hz* T⁻¹* 13-23* decision content* D_a* 1* 1* 13-24* information content* I(x)* Sh* 1* 13-25* entropy* H* Sh* 1* 13-26* maximum entropy* H_0* Sh* 1* 13-27* relative entropy* H_r* 1* 1* 13-28* redundancy* R* Sh* 1* 13-29* relative redundancy* r* 1* 1* 13-30* joint information content* I(x,y)* Sh* 1* 13-31* conditional information content* I(x|y)* Sh* 1* 13-32* conditional entropy* H(X|Y)* Sh* 1* 13-33* equivocation* H_x(X|Y)* Sh* 1* 13-34* irrelevance* H_y(Y|X)* Sh* 1* 13-35* transinformation content* T(x,y)* Sh* 1* 13-36* mean transinformation content* T* Sh* 1* 13-37* character mean entropy* H′* Sh* 1* 13-38* average information rate* H** Sh/s* T⁻¹* 13-39* character mean transinformation content* T′* Sh* 1* 13-40* average transinformation rate* T** Sh/s* T⁻¹* 13-41* channel capacity per character* C′* Sh* 1* 13-42* channel time capacity* C** Sh/s* T⁻¹\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009IEC 80000-13 — Information science and technology","header":"31.5. Item index — IEC 80000-13","id":"/IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology/Item-index-___-IEC-80000-13/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--IEC-80000-13-___-Information-science-and-technology--Item-index-___-IEC-80000-13"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Synopsis--Purpose":{"contents":"Open each oscillator into its body and wall spring, add a coupling spring between bodies A and B, and treat the coupled pair as a system of its own sort, pairS. assemble sums a quantity of kind k over the parts of a whole of sort σ only if an Assembles σ k instance exists; the kind alone does not license the sum. The lesson provides Assembles instances for mass over a pair's bodies and for stiffness over springs in parallel. It provides none for angular frequency over a pair or for stiffness over springs in series, so those sums fail to type-check.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.3.1.1. Purpose","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Synopsis--Purpose"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Purpose":{"contents":"State what must hold of the refined context as requirements that the build checks. Each Provenance.Requirement names the boundary it governs (a Provenance.Contract), the output port it is about, and its scope, the objects it quantifies over. R1 and R2 are scoped to the sort pairS: R1 is proved by a theorem, and R2 holds because no Assembles instance exists. R3 is scoped to pair P and is decided by a gate function over every state of P. The coverage census lists every output port of every boundary with the requirements that govern it, and #kind_requirement_clean fails if any port is ungoverned or unchecked.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.7.1.1. Purpose","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Purpose"}});